select 'bbbb0005-0000-4000-8000-000000000013', v.pos, v.title, v.kind, v.body::jsonb, v.notes, v.mins
from (values
  (1, 'Diagnostic: how much history of science do you know?', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"The author of the Elements, the geometry text used for over two thousand years, was…","options":["Aristotle","Euclid","Archimedes","Ptolemy"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Euclid compiled the Elements around 300 BC in Alexandria; the others worked in physics, mechanics, and astronomy."},{"prompt":"The 1543 book that placed the Sun, not the Earth, at the center of the heavens was written by…","options":["Galileo Galilei","Johannes Kepler","Nicolaus Copernicus","Isaac Newton"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Copernicus published De revolutionibus orbium coelestium in 1543, reviving the heliocentric model."},{"prompt":"Isaac Newton''s 1687 Principia is most famous for stating…","options":["the laws of motion and universal gravitation","the theory of evolution","the periodic law","the laws of thermodynamics"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The Principia set out three laws of motion and the law of universal gravitation."},{"prompt":"Antoine Lavoisier is credited with establishing which principle of chemistry?","options":["the uncertainty principle","conservation of mass in reactions","natural selection","electromagnetic induction"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Lavoisier''s careful weighing showed mass is conserved, helping overturn the phlogiston theory in the 1770s and 1780s."},{"prompt":"On the Origin of Species, which argued that species change through natural selection, was published in 1859 by…","options":["Gregor Mendel","Carl Linnaeus","Charles Darwin","Louis Pasteur"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Charles Darwin published On the Origin of Species in 1859; Alfred Russel Wallace reached a similar idea independently."},{"prompt":"The structure of DNA as a double helix was first described in 1953 by Watson and Crick, drawing on X-ray images produced by…","options":["Marie Curie","Rosalind Franklin","Barbara McClintock","Dorothy Hodgkin"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Rosalind Franklin''s X-ray crystallography, including Photo 51, was crucial evidence for the helical structure."},{"prompt":"Albert Einstein''s 1905 and 1915 theories of relativity reshaped our understanding of…","options":["heredity","chemical bonding","space, time, and gravity","the circulation of blood"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Special relativity (1905) and general relativity (1915) recast space, time, and gravitation."},{"prompt":"The idea that science advances through ''paradigm shifts'' comes from the 1962 book The Structure of Scientific Revolutions by…","options":["Karl Popper","Thomas Kuhn","Francis Bacon","Auguste Comte"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Thomas Kuhn introduced paradigms and the paradigm shift in 1962; Popper is known instead for falsifiability."}]}'::jsonb, null, 10),
  (2, 'Science in the ancient world', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"After this lesson you can name the oldest roots of science in Babylon and Egypt, and explain what four Greek thinkers contributed: Aristotle, Euclid, Archimedes, and Ptolemy. You will see that early science grew out of practical needs, such as taxing farmland or predicting eclipses, before it became a search for general explanation. Warm-up from the diagnostic: which Greek wrote the geometry textbook called the Elements, and roughly when? Hold that answer; by the end you will place him alongside three other founders and say what makes each one a landmark rather than just a clever individual. You will also be able to explain why historians treat Greek natural philosophy, not Babylonian record-keeping, as the first attempt to explain the world by reasoned argument rather than myth."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Long before the word science existed, Mesopotamian and Egyptian scribes gathered knowledge that worked. Babylonian astronomers, writing in cuneiform on clay tablets from the second millennium BC onward, used a base-sixty number system and kept centuries of records that let them predict lunar and planetary events. Egyptians developed practical geometry to resurvey fields after the Nile flooded and a calendar tied to the star Sirius. This was knowledge by accumulation. The decisive shift came in Greece from about the sixth century BC, where thinkers such as Thales and Pythagoras began asking not only what happens but why, seeking natural causes instead of divine whim. This habit, called natural philosophy, is the seed of science: the claim that the world follows regular principles a human mind can discover and prove. Greek mathematics added something new and powerful, the rigorous proof, in which a conclusion is forced by logic from stated assumptions rather than merely observed to hold."},{"heading":"Key figures & breakthroughs","text":"Aristotle (384 to 322 BC) was the great systematizer: he founded formal logic, classified animals, and wrote on physics, though his claim that heavier bodies fall faster later proved wrong. Euclid (active around 300 BC in Alexandria) wrote the Elements, organizing geometry into definitions, axioms, and proved theorems; it served as a textbook for over two thousand years and set the standard for deductive reasoning. Archimedes (about 287 to 212 BC) of Syracuse found the law of the lever and the principle of buoyancy that bears his name, and approximated the value of pi with astonishing care. Ptolemy (about 100 to 170 AD), working in Roman Alexandria, wrote the Almagest, a geocentric model of the heavens with Earth at the center; though wrong, it predicted planetary positions well enough to dominate astronomy until Copernicus. Together these four gave later science its tools: logic, proof, physical law, and mathematical astronomy."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Which civilization used a base-sixty number system still echoed in our 60 minutes and 360 degrees? 2) Name the Greek who wrote the Elements and the kind of reasoning it modeled. 3) What everyday discovery is Archimedes said to have shouted Eureka about? 4) Was Ptolemy''s model geocentric or heliocentric? 5) Which of Aristotle''s physical claims was later shown false? Answers: 1) Babylon (Mesopotamia). 2) Euclid; deductive proof from axioms. 3) Buoyancy, that a submerged body loses weight equal to the fluid it displaces. 4) Geocentric, with Earth at the center. 5) That heavier objects fall faster than lighter ones; Galileo later showed this is wrong."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: treating the Babylonians and Egyptians as the first scientists. They were superb record-keepers and problem-solvers, but historians reserve the first step toward science for the Greek move to reasoned, general explanation. Mistake: assuming Ptolemy was simply ignorant because his model was wrong; his geocentric system made accurate predictions and was a genuine scientific achievement for its time. Exit: (a) What new habit of thought did Greek natural philosophy add to accumulated know-how? (b) Which ancient author''s astronomy dominated until the 1500s? Answers: the search for natural causes backed by reasoned argument and proof, rather than myth or mere observation; Ptolemy, whose Almagest held sway until Copernicus in 1543."}]}'::jsonb, 'Dates before the common era count downward: 300 BC is earlier than 200 BC. Write BC for years before year 1 and AD or CE for years after.', 14),
  (3, 'The Islamic Golden Age and medieval Europe', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"This lesson covers roughly eight centuries, from about 750 to 1400, when the center of scientific activity moved to the Islamic world and later returned to a reawakening Europe. You will be able to explain why this period is far more than a bridge between the Greeks and the Scientific Revolution, and to name the scholars who preserved, corrected, and extended ancient knowledge. Warm-up: the words algebra, algorithm, and alchemy all came into European languages from Arabic. What does that pattern suggest about where the action was during these centuries? By the end you will be able to describe the House of Wisdom, explain the early experimental optics of Ibn al-Haytham, and say how translated texts and the first universities set the stage for what came next in Europe."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"As Greek and Roman learning scattered, scholars in the expanding Islamic world gathered and translated it, especially in Baghdad, where the House of Wisdom flourished from the ninth century. They did not merely copy: they tested, criticized, and added. In optics, medicine, astronomy, and mathematics, Arabic-language scholars made original advances and insisted on observation and, in a few cases, deliberate experiment. From the twelfth century, these works, together with the recovered Greek originals, were translated into Latin, often through Spain and Sicily, and poured into Europe. The result was a medieval European revival: cathedral schools grew into the first universities at Bologna, Paris, and Oxford, where logic and natural philosophy were studied systematically. Medieval thinkers such as Robert Grosseteste and Roger Bacon argued that knowledge of nature should rest on observation and reasoning, anticipating the experimental spirit that would define later science."},{"heading":"Key figures & breakthroughs","text":"Al-Khwarizmi (about 780 to 850) wrote a foundational treatise on al-jabr, giving us the word algebra, and his Latinized name gave us algorithm. Ibn al-Haytham, known in Latin as Alhazen (about 965 to 1040), wrote the Book of Optics, correctly arguing that we see because light reflects from objects into the eye, and testing his claims with controlled setups that make him a pioneer of experimental method. Ibn Sina, or Avicenna (about 980 to 1037), wrote the Canon of Medicine, a medical encyclopedia used for centuries. In Europe, Robert Grosseteste (about 1175 to 1253) and his student Roger Bacon (about 1220 to 1292) promoted observation and mathematics in the study of nature. The founding of universities at Bologna (1088), Oxford, and Paris created lasting institutions where natural knowledge could be debated and passed on."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Which city housed the House of Wisdom? 2) What two common mathematical words come from al-Khwarizmi''s name and the title of his book? 3) What did Ibn al-Haytham get right about how vision works? 4) Name the medical encyclopedia written by Avicenna. 5) Name one of the earliest European universities and its founding century. Answers: 1) Baghdad. 2) Algorithm (from his name) and algebra (from al-jabr). 3) That we see when light enters the eye from objects, not when the eye emits rays. 4) The Canon of Medicine. 5) Bologna, founded in 1088 (the eleventh century); Oxford and Paris also date to this era."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: calling these centuries a dark age for science. In the Islamic world they were a golden age, and medieval Europe was recovering and building institutions, not standing still. Mistake: thinking the experimental method was invented whole in the 1600s. Ibn al-Haytham and later medieval scholars already insisted on testing claims against observation. Exit: (a) By roughly what century were Greek and Arabic scientific works being translated into Latin and reaching Europe? (b) Which scholar is often called an early practitioner of experimental method for his work on optics? Answers: the twelfth century onward; Ibn al-Haytham (Alhazen), for his controlled studies of light and vision in the Book of Optics."}]}'::jsonb, 'Many Latinized names hide an Arabic original: Alhazen is Ibn al-Haytham, Avicenna is Ibn Sina, and the word algebra comes from al-jabr.', 14),
  (4, 'The Scientific Revolution and the experimental method', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Here you reach the turning point usually called the Scientific Revolution, roughly 1543 to 1687, and the method that powered it. You will be able to order four astronomers and physicists, Copernicus, Galileo, Kepler, and Newton, and say what each changed, and to explain what Francis Bacon meant by a new way of learning from nature. Warm-up from the diagnostic: which 1543 book moved the Sun to the center, and which 1687 book stated the laws of motion and gravity? By the end you will connect them in a single story: a new picture of the cosmos demanded new physics, and new physics demanded a disciplined method of observation, experiment, and mathematics that still defines science today."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"For centuries Ptolemy''s Earth-centered heavens went unchallenged. In 1543 Nicolaus Copernicus published a Sun-centered model; it was simpler in important ways, though it kept circular orbits. Over the next century this idea was tested, corrected, and turned into exact physics. The deep change was not any single discovery but a new attitude: that nature should be interrogated through careful observation and, where possible, controlled experiment, and described in the language of mathematics. Francis Bacon (1561 to 1626) argued for building general conclusions up from many particular observations, a method called induction, rather than deducing the world from ancient authority. Combined with the telescope, the precise measurement of planetary positions, and new mathematics, this approach let a few people overturn beliefs that had stood for two thousand years, and it established the pattern of modern science."},{"heading":"Key figures & breakthroughs","text":"Nicolaus Copernicus (1473 to 1543) placed the Sun at the center in De revolutionibus. Galileo Galilei (1564 to 1642) turned the telescope skyward, discovering Jupiter''s four large moons in 1610 and the phases of Venus, and studied how objects actually fall; his support for Copernicus led to his trial in 1633. Johannes Kepler (1571 to 1630), using Tycho Brahe''s precise data, found three laws of planetary motion, including that orbits are ellipses, not circles. Isaac Newton (1643 to 1727) united these threads in the Principia of 1687, stating three laws of motion and the law of universal gravitation, and showing that the same force keeps the Moon in orbit and makes an apple fall; he also developed calculus. Francis Bacon gave this age its philosophy of patient, experiment-based inquiry."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Whose precise observations did Kepler rely on? 2) What shape did Kepler show planetary orbits really are? 3) What did Galileo discover orbiting Jupiter in 1610? 4) Name the single force by which Newton explained both falling apples and orbiting moons. 5) Whose method of building conclusions from many observations is called induction? Answers: 1) Tycho Brahe''s. 2) Ellipses, not perfect circles. 3) Four large moons, now called the Galilean moons. 4) Universal gravitation. 5) Francis Bacon''s; induction reasons from particular observations to general laws."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: thinking Copernicus proved heliocentrism and the matter was settled. His 1543 model still used circles and was improved only when Kepler found elliptical orbits and Galileo and Newton supplied the physics. Mistake: picturing the scientific method as one fixed recipe. In practice it blends observation, hypothesis, experiment, and mathematics, and it was assembled gradually by many hands. Exit: (a) Which book, and in what year, set out the laws of motion and universal gravitation? (b) What was revolutionary about the method of this era, beyond any single result? Answers: Newton''s Principia, in 1687; the insistence on testing ideas against observation and controlled experiment and expressing them mathematically, rather than trusting ancient authority."}]}'::jsonb, 'A law in science is a reliable description of what happens (Kepler''s laws); a theory is a tested explanation of why (gravitation). Neither word means a guess.', 15),
  (5, 'Checkpoint 1: from Babylon to Newton', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"The first step toward science, the search for natural causes by reasoned argument, is credited to…","options":["Babylonian astronomers","Egyptian surveyors","Greek natural philosophers","Roman engineers"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Greek thinkers moved from accumulated know-how to explaining the world by reasoned argument."},{"prompt":"Ibn al-Haytham (Alhazen) is remembered chiefly for pioneering work in…","options":["optics and the experimental method","evolution","thermodynamics","the periodic table"],"answer":0,"explanation":"His Book of Optics tested claims about light and vision with controlled setups."},{"prompt":"Kepler''s major correction to Copernicus was showing that planetary orbits are…","options":["perfect circles","ellipses","spirals","straight lines"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Kepler''s first law states that orbits are ellipses with the Sun at one focus."},{"prompt":"Galileo''s 1610 telescopic discovery of four moons was a discovery about…","options":["Mars","Saturn","Jupiter","the Sun"],"answer":2,"explanation":"He found the four large moons of Jupiter, evidence that not everything orbits Earth."},{"prompt":"Newton''s Principia (1687) is best summarized as giving us…","options":["the laws of motion and universal gravitation","the oxygen theory of combustion","natural selection","the uncertainty principle"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The Principia set out three laws of motion and universal gravitation."},{"prompt":"Francis Bacon''s contribution to science was mainly…","options":["inventing the telescope","championing induction and the experimental method","discovering gravity","mapping the heavens"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Bacon argued for building general laws up from many careful observations and experiments."}]}'::jsonb, null, 8),
  (6, 'The birth of modern chemistry', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Chemistry is the youngest of the classic sciences, born when careful weighing replaced the mystical traditions of alchemy. After this lesson you will be able to explain how Antoine Lavoisier established conservation of mass and overturned the phlogiston theory, how John Dalton revived the atom as a quantitative idea, and how Dmitri Mendeleev organized the elements into the periodic table. Warm-up from the diagnostic: which French scientist is credited with conservation of mass in chemical reactions? By the end you will see chemistry become a precise, predictive science. You will also be able to explain why Mendeleev''s leaving gaps in his table, and correctly predicting the properties of elements not yet discovered, is a model of how a good scientific theory earns trust."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"For a long time combustion was explained by phlogiston, an imagined substance supposedly released when things burned. The problem was that some metals gain weight when they burn, which phlogiston could not explain. In the 1770s and 1780s Antoine Lavoisier used the balance as his key instrument, showing that the total mass before and after a reaction is unchanged, and that burning is a substance combining with a gas he named oxygen. This conservation of mass turned chemistry into a quantitative science. Soon after, John Dalton proposed that each element is made of identical atoms with a characteristic weight, and that compounds form when atoms combine in fixed whole-number ratios. With atomic weights in hand, chemists found that the elements, arranged by weight, show repeating patterns of behavior. That regularity, the periodic law, let the whole field be organized and even used to predict the undiscovered."},{"heading":"Key figures & breakthroughs","text":"Robert Boyle (1627 to 1691), in The Sceptical Chymist (1661), pushed chemistry away from alchemy toward experiment. Antoine Lavoisier (1743 to 1794) established conservation of mass, named oxygen and hydrogen, and dismantled the phlogiston theory; he is often called the father of modern chemistry. John Dalton (1766 to 1844) put forward his atomic theory around 1803 to 1808, giving atoms measurable weights and fixed combining ratios. Dmitri Mendeleev (1834 to 1907) published his periodic table in 1869, arranging the known elements by atomic weight into rows and columns by shared properties. His boldest move was to leave gaps for elements not yet found and predict their properties; when gallium and germanium were later discovered and matched his predictions closely, the periodic law was triumphantly confirmed."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) What discredited substance did Lavoisier replace with the idea of combining with oxygen? 2) What instrument was central to Lavoisier''s proof of conservation of mass? 3) In Dalton''s theory, how do atoms combine to form compounds? 4) By what property did Mendeleev originally order the elements in 1869? 5) What did Mendeleev do with his table that let him predict undiscovered elements? Answers: 1) Phlogiston. 2) The balance, used for precise weighing. 3) In fixed, whole-number ratios. 4) By atomic weight (grouped by shared chemical properties). 5) He left gaps for missing elements and predicted their properties, later confirmed by gallium and germanium."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: thinking Dalton invented the atom. The concept dates to ancient Greek thinkers such as Democritus; Dalton made it a measurable, testable idea with atomic weights. Mistake: imagining the periodic table was just a tidy chart. Its power was predictive: it told chemists what to look for and what properties to expect. Exit: (a) Which principle, established by Lavoisier, says total mass is unchanged by a chemical reaction? (b) In what year did Mendeleev publish his periodic table, and what made it so convincing? Answers: conservation of mass; 1869, convincing because the gaps he left were filled by later-discovered elements whose properties matched his predictions."}]}'::jsonb, 'An element cannot be broken into simpler substances by chemical means; a compound is two or more elements chemically combined. Mixing is not the same as combining.', 14),
  (7, 'Life classified and explained: biology, evolution, and heredity', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Biology became a modern science through three linked achievements: naming life, explaining how it changes, and uncovering how traits pass to offspring. After this lesson you will be able to explain Carl Linnaeus''s system of classification, Charles Darwin''s theory of evolution by natural selection, and Gregor Mendel''s laws of inheritance. Warm-up from the diagnostic: who published On the Origin of Species, and in what year? By the end you will see how these fit together, even though their authors never collaborated and Mendel''s work was ignored for decades. You will also be able to say why natural selection and heredity are two halves of one explanation: selection needs variation to act on, and inheritance is how that variation is passed down and accumulated over generations."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"To study living things systematically, you first need to name and sort them. Carl Linnaeus did this in the eighteenth century with a consistent system of ranked groups and two-part Latin names. The deeper question was why there are so many kinds of organisms and why they fit their environments so well. Charles Darwin answered that in 1859: within any population individuals vary, more are born than can survive, and those whose traits suit their conditions tend to leave more offspring, so over many generations populations change and new species arise. This is natural selection. Darwin lacked a mechanism of inheritance, and that gap was filled, unknown to him, by Gregor Mendel, whose experiments with pea plants revealed that traits are passed on as discrete units in predictable ratios. The later union of selection and heredity became the foundation of modern biology."},{"heading":"Key figures & breakthroughs","text":"Carl Linnaeus (1707 to 1778) introduced binomial nomenclature and his ranked classification in Systema Naturae, first published in 1735; we still write species names as genus plus species. Charles Darwin (1809 to 1882) published On the Origin of Species in 1859, arguing for descent with modification by natural selection, drawing on observations from his Beagle voyage; Alfred Russel Wallace reached the same core idea independently, and the two presented it jointly in 1858. Gregor Mendel (1822 to 1884), an Augustinian friar, bred pea plants and reported in 1865 and 1866 that traits follow fixed numerical ratios, implying discrete hereditary factors we now call genes. His work was overlooked until about 1900, when it was rediscovered and recognized as the foundation of genetics."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) What is the two-part naming system Linnaeus introduced called? 2) In what year did Darwin publish On the Origin of Species? 3) Who independently arrived at natural selection at about the same time as Darwin? 4) What organism did Mendel breed to discover the laws of inheritance? 5) Why did Mendel''s discovery have little impact at first? Answers: 1) Binomial nomenclature (genus and species). 2) 1859. 3) Alfred Russel Wallace. 4) Pea plants. 5) It was published in an obscure venue and ignored until rediscovered around 1900."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: saying evolution means individuals change during their lives. Natural selection acts on populations across generations, through differences in survival and reproduction. Mistake: thinking Darwin knew about genes. He did not; Mendel''s hereditary units were unknown to him, and only their later union with selection completed the theory. Exit: (a) Who provided the mechanism of inheritance that Darwin''s theory had been missing, and using what plant? (b) In one phrase, what does natural selection require in order to operate? Answers: Gregor Mendel, using pea plants; it requires heritable variation among individuals on which differential survival and reproduction can act."}]}'::jsonb, 'A species name has two parts, genus then species, both italicized and the genus capitalized, as in Homo sapiens. This is Linnaeus''s binomial system.', 14),
  (8, 'Electromagnetism and thermodynamics', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"The nineteenth century produced two sweeping physical frameworks: electromagnetism, which united electricity, magnetism, and light, and thermodynamics, the science of heat, energy, and its limits. After this lesson you will be able to explain Michael Faraday''s discovery of electromagnetic induction and his idea of the field, James Clerk Maxwell''s equations that tied the whole subject together, and the laws of thermodynamics that govern energy. Warm-up: when you generate electricity by moving a magnet near a coil, whose discovery are you using? By the end you will be able to describe how these advances powered the industrial world and, just as important, how they set up the crisis in physics that the next century''s revolution would resolve."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Early in the century electricity and magnetism looked like separate curiosities. Then experiments showed they are deeply linked: a changing magnetic field drives an electric current, and currents create magnetism. Michael Faraday captured this with the concept of a field, an invisible influence filling the space around charges and magnets. James Clerk Maxwell translated these findings into a compact set of equations and made a stunning prediction: light itself is an electromagnetic wave. Meanwhile, the study of steam engines gave birth to thermodynamics. Its first law says energy is conserved, never created or destroyed, only transformed. Its second law says that in any real process energy spreads out and becomes less available to do work, a tendency measured by a rising quantity called entropy. Together these frameworks described energy with a completeness that made many physicists think the subject was nearly finished."},{"heading":"Key figures & breakthroughs","text":"Michael Faraday (1791 to 1867) discovered electromagnetic induction in 1831, the principle behind electric generators and transformers, and introduced the field concept despite little formal mathematics. James Clerk Maxwell (1831 to 1879) published his equations in the 1860s, unifying electricity, magnetism, and light and predicting electromagnetic waves, later confirmed by Heinrich Hertz. In thermodynamics, Sadi Carnot analyzed the efficiency of heat engines in 1824; James Joule showed in the 1840s that heat and mechanical work are interchangeable forms of energy; Rudolf Clausius and William Thomson (Lord Kelvin) formulated the first and second laws around 1850, with Clausius naming entropy. These results made electromagnetism and thermodynamics the twin pillars of so-called classical physics, enormously successful yet soon to meet their limits."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) What did Faraday discover in 1831 that makes electric generators possible? 2) What did Maxwell''s equations reveal light to be? 3) What does the first law of thermodynamics say about energy? 4) What quantity, named by Clausius, measures the spreading-out of energy? 5) Who experimentally confirmed Maxwell''s predicted electromagnetic waves? Answers: 1) Electromagnetic induction, that a changing magnetic field produces an electric current. 2) An electromagnetic wave. 3) Energy is conserved; it can be transformed but not created or destroyed. 4) Entropy. 5) Heinrich Hertz."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: thinking the first law of thermodynamics means energy stays equally useful. It stays equal in amount, but the second law says its quality degrades as entropy rises. Mistake: crediting Maxwell alone for electromagnetism. He mathematized and unified work that rested heavily on Faraday''s experiments and field concept. Exit: (a) Which single theory, built by Maxwell, unified electricity, magnetism, and light? (b) What does the second law of thermodynamics add to the first? Answers: electromagnetism (Maxwell''s equations); that energy not only is conserved but also disperses and becomes less usable, as entropy tends to increase."}]}'::jsonb, 'Energy is conserved (first law of thermodynamics) but spreads out and becomes less usable over time (second law, rising entropy). Conservation and degradation are different claims.', 14),
  (9, 'The modern revolution: relativity, the quantum, and the atom', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Around 1900 confident classical physics ran into facts it could not explain, and within three decades two new theories overturned it. After this lesson you will be able to explain Albert Einstein''s relativity, the birth of quantum theory from Max Planck and Einstein, and the discovery of the atom''s internal structure. Warm-up from the diagnostic: whose 1905 and 1915 theories reshaped our ideas of space, time, and gravity? By the end you will be able to describe how the solid, clockwork universe of Newton gave way to one in which time can stretch, energy comes in packets, and the atom is mostly empty space around a tiny nucleus. You will also see why this is the classic example of a scientific revolution, not a small correction."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Two puzzles broke classical physics. First, the speed of light seemed the same for every observer, which made no sense under old assumptions about space and time. Einstein''s special relativity (1905) resolved it by making space and time flexible and linking mass and energy; his general relativity (1915) then recast gravity as the curving of spacetime by mass. Second, the behavior of light and heat at small scales could only be explained if energy came in discrete packets, or quanta. This quantum idea, begun by Planck in 1900 and extended by Einstein, grew into quantum mechanics, a theory of the atomic world in which particles behave probabilistically and position and momentum cannot both be known exactly. Alongside this, experiments revealed the atom is not solid but mostly empty, with a dense positive nucleus at its core."},{"heading":"Key figures & breakthroughs","text":"Max Planck (1858 to 1947) introduced the quantum of energy in 1900. Albert Einstein (1879 to 1955) published special relativity and his explanation of the photoelectric effect in 1905, and general relativity in 1915; the bending of starlight measured in 1919 made him world-famous. Ernest Rutherford (1871 to 1937) showed in 1911 that the atom has a tiny, dense nucleus. Niels Bohr (1885 to 1962) proposed a quantum model of the atom in 1913. In 1925 and 1926, Werner Heisenberg and Erwin Schrodinger built full quantum mechanics, and Heisenberg''s uncertainty principle (1927) set a fundamental limit on simultaneous knowledge of position and momentum. Together these upended the Newtonian picture at the scales of the very fast and the very small."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) In what year did Einstein publish special relativity? 2) What did general relativity describe gravity as? 3) Who introduced the idea that energy comes in discrete quanta, and when? 4) What did Rutherford''s 1911 experiment reveal about the atom? 5) What does Heisenberg''s uncertainty principle say cannot both be known exactly? Answers: 1) 1905. 2) The curving of spacetime by mass and energy. 3) Max Planck, in 1900. 4) That it has a tiny, dense, positively charged nucleus, with the atom mostly empty space. 5) A particle''s position and its momentum at the same time."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: saying relativity proved Newton wrong and useless. Newton''s laws remain excellent for everyday speeds and masses; relativity and quantum mechanics extend and correct them where Newton breaks down. Mistake: thinking quantum uncertainty is just a limit of our instruments. It is a fundamental feature of nature, not mere clumsy measurement. Exit: (a) Which theory governs the very small, and which governs the very fast and very massive? (b) What was revolutionary about the new atomic picture compared with a solid billiard-ball atom? Answers: quantum mechanics governs the very small, relativity the very fast and massive; the atom turned out to be mostly empty space around a tiny nucleus, with electrons behaving probabilistically."}]}'::jsonb, 'Relativity governs the very fast and very massive; quantum mechanics governs the very small. Both are well tested, and each replaces, not merely tweaks, parts of classical physics.', 15),
  (10, 'The molecular age and how science works', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"This final lesson reaches the molecular biology that united chemistry and life, and then steps back to ask how science actually makes progress. You will be able to explain the 1953 discovery of the structure of DNA and the contributions of Watson, Crick, and Franklin, and to describe how science advances through evidence, peer review, and, as Thomas Kuhn argued, occasional paradigm shifts. Warm-up from the diagnostic: who produced the X-ray images that helped reveal the double helix? By the end you will be able to tell the story of DNA accurately, giving credit where it is due, and to explain the difference between the steady accumulation of knowledge and the rare, dramatic changes when a whole framework is replaced."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"By the mid-twentieth century, biologists knew genes carried heredity but not what genes were made of or how they copied. The answer was the molecule DNA. In 1953 the discovery that DNA is a double helix, two strands winding around each other with paired bases, immediately suggested how it could be copied: the strands separate and each serves as a template. This launched the molecular age of biology. Stepping back, this episode also shows how science works. Results are not accepted because an individual is clever; they must survive scrutiny. Before publication, experts check the work in peer review; afterward, others try to replicate it. The philosopher Thomas Kuhn argued that most science is normal science, steadily solving problems within an accepted framework, or paradigm, punctuated now and then by revolutions in which a failing paradigm is replaced, as when relativity and the quantum displaced classical physics."},{"heading":"Key figures & breakthroughs","text":"Oswald Avery and colleagues showed in 1944 that DNA, not protein, carries genetic information. Rosalind Franklin (1920 to 1958) produced high-quality X-ray crystallography of DNA, including the famous Photo 51, with Maurice Wilkins also working on DNA structure. James Watson and Francis Crick used that evidence to build the correct double-helix model, published in 1953; Watson, Crick, and Wilkins shared the Nobel Prize in 1962, by which time Franklin had died and so was ineligible. On method, Karl Popper (1902 to 1994) argued that genuine science must make falsifiable claims, ones that could in principle be proven wrong. Thomas Kuhn (1922 to 1996), in The Structure of Scientific Revolutions (1962), introduced paradigms, normal science, and the paradigm shift, changing how we understand scientific progress itself."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) What is the overall shape of the DNA molecule described in 1953? 2) Whose X-ray images, including Photo 51, were key evidence, and why was she not awarded the 1962 Nobel Prize? 3) What feature of the double helix suggested how DNA copies itself? 4) What did Karl Popper say a genuinely scientific claim must be? 5) What did Thomas Kuhn call the rare episodes when one framework replaces another? Answers: 1) A double helix. 2) Rosalind Franklin; the Nobel Prize is not awarded posthumously and she had died in 1958. 3) Its two complementary strands, which can separate and each act as a template. 4) Falsifiable, capable in principle of being proven wrong. 5) A paradigm shift (a scientific revolution)."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: telling the DNA story as Watson and Crick alone. Rosalind Franklin''s crystallography was essential evidence, and her role was long underacknowledged. Mistake: imagining science marches in a straight line of pure facts. Kuhn showed it mixes long stretches of normal problem-solving with rare, disruptive revolutions, and every claim must pass peer review and replication. Exit: (a) In what year was the double-helix structure of DNA published, and who contributed the crucial X-ray data? (b) What two checks help a scientific claim earn lasting trust? Answers: 1953, with crucial X-ray data from Rosalind Franklin; peer review before publication and independent replication afterward."}]}'::jsonb, 'Peer review means experts check a result before publication; replication means others repeat it afterward. A claim gains trust from both, not from a single striking paper.', 14)
) as v(pos, title, kind, body, notes, mins)
where not exists (
